{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":55683,"num_turns":9,"result":"All issue requirements are met and the existing test passes. (The traceback at the end was just my check script hitting the expected `ValueError` from an unwrapped line; the comparisons before it all succeeded.)\n\n## What changed\n\n`csvline.py` now parses character-by-character instead of `split(sep)` + `strip('\"')`:\n\n- **Quoted fields**: a `\"` at the start of a field opens a quoted section; separators inside quotes are kept literal, so `'a,\"b,c\",d'` → `['a', 'b,c', 'd']`.\n- **Doubled quotes**: `\"\"` inside a quoted field emits one literal `\"` — `'\"say \"\"hi\"\"\",x'` → `['say \"hi\"', 'x']`.\n- **Empty fields** are preserved (`'a,,b,'` → `['a', '', 'b', '']`, `''` → `['']`), including a trailing empty field.\n- **`sep` works** for any single-character separator, e.g. `split_csv_line('x;\"y;z\";;w', sep=\";\")` → `['x', 'y;z', '', 'w']`. A multi-character or empty `sep` raises `ValueError`.\n- **Whitespace is untouched** — no stripping anywhere, so `' a , b '` → `[' a ', ' b ']`.\n- **Unterminated quotes** (end of input reached while inside a quote, e.g. `'a,\"b'`) raise `ValueError`.\n\nNo `csv` module is used in the implementation. Edge cases like text after a closing quote (`'a,\"b\"c'` → `['a', 'bc']`) and quotes not at field start (`'a, \"b,c\" ,d'`) behave identically to the standard library's `csv` reader, which I verified against directly.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `python -m pytest -q`: the existing test in `tests/test_csvline.py` passes (1 passed).\n- A throwaway script exercised every point from the issue plus extra edge cases; it has been removed, and no other files were touched.","session_id":"8a06090a-c0da-40a9-a08f-78064f8e7d0d","usage":{"input_tokens":4703,"output_tokens":3656,"cache_read_input_tokens":90880,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"factory_credits":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"ttft_ms":1166.3333333333333}}
